"── 103_winui_ghost.mst — WG5c D5: the ghost line, for real ──────────────
 docs/sprints/sprint_wg5c_detail.md D5, and §2.1's Workspace row weakness
 #4: one ghosted line on an empty Workspace, telling a user who has never
 met a Smalltalk workspace what the thing wants from them.

 WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL: WG5a D4 shipped it broken. `EM_SETCUEBANNER`
 is documented for SINGLE-LINE edit controls and returns 0 — refused — on a
 multiline one. Nothing drew, nothing complained, and the test asserted the
 TEXT the ghost would use rather than that anything appeared. So this file
 is a re-implementation, and the rule that comes with it is: CHECK IT ON
 SCREEN.

 THE DESIGN, and the reason it is not the obvious one. The obvious approach
 is to put the ghost in the pane as real greyed text and clear it on the
 first keystroke. That makes the ghost part of the DOCUMENT, and the
 document is what Do It evaluates and what Accept writes — so a user who
 pressed Ctrl-D on an untouched Workspace would evaluate the hint, and any
 bug in the `is the ghost showing?` flag would either evaluate it or discard
 real typing.

 So the ghost is an OVERLAY: a STATIC sibling raised above the pane, shown
 only while the pane is empty. `text` stays empty because the ghost is not
 text. It cannot be evaluated, cannot be saved, and cannot be lost — the
 failure modes are gone by construction rather than guarded against."

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: GhostVisible>

    WinShell class >> ghostName ^ <Symbol> [ ^#workspaceGhost ]

    WinShell class >> buildGhost [
        "A STATIC, because it draws text and takes no focus. SS_NOTIFY so a
         click on it can be forwarded to the pane underneath — without it the
         hint is a dead zone exactly where a new user is most likely to
         click, which would teach them the opposite of what it says."
        (self controlNamed: self ghostName) ifNotNil: [ :c | ^self ].
        self
            addControl: self ghostName
            class: 'STATIC'
            text: self ghostText
            "SS_OWNERDRAW, so the hint is drawn GREY. A plain STATIC uses the
             dialog text colour, which made the hint read as code the user had
             not typed rather than as a hint — arguably worse than no hint at
             all, since it invites a Do It on text that is not in the buffer.
             Colouring a stock static means handling WM_CTLCOLORSTATIC, a
             message the door does not carry; owner-draw reuses the
             WM_DRAWITEM path WG4 D3 already built and proved."
            style: ((((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SS_OWNERDRAW'))
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SS_NOTIFY'))
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_CLIPSIBLINGS'))
            exStyle: 0.
        (self controlNamed: self ghostName) ifNotNil: [ :c | c setFont: self monoFont ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> ghostRectIn: content <Array> ^ <Array> [
        "Inset to sit where the pane's first line of text sits. A RichEdit
         leaves a small internal margin and a border of its own; matching it
         by eye is what makes the hint look like text IN the pane rather than
         a label stuck on top of it.

         Pure arithmetic over the content rect, so it is testable with no
         window — the same treatment every other rect in this shell gets."
        ^Array
            with: (content at: 1) + 3
            with: (content at: 2) + 2
            with: ((content at: 3) - 6 max: 0)
            with: 20
    ]

    WinShell class >> layoutGhost: content <Array> [
        (self controlNamed: self ghostName) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            c moveTo: (self ghostRectIn: content).
            "Raise it ABOVE the pane. Sibling z-order is creation order, and
             the pane may well have been created after the ghost — so being
             on top has to be asked for rather than assumed."
            WinApi setWindowPos: c handle after: 0
                x: 0 y: 0 width: 0 height: 0
                flags: (((WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOMOVE')
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOSIZE'))
                    bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOACTIVATE')) ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── How it draws ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Here rather than in 94 for a compile-time reason: this method names
     WinSyntax, which 100 defines, and 94 loads before 100. `drawItem:` in 94
     reaches it by MESSAGE SEND, which is resolved at run time and so has no
     such constraint — the same distinction that moved the source pane's
     image read from 98 to 99."

    WinShell class >> drawGhost: hdc <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                      w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "WG5c D5's hint, in the pane's own background with GREY text. The grey
         is what makes it read as a hint rather than as content — the whole
         point of the word ghost.

         The background is filled explicitly rather than left transparent:
         the overlay sits on top of a RichEdit, and letting the pane show
         through would put the hint over whatever the pane last drew there."
        | rect brush old |
        rect := self drawRectScratch.
        self setRect: rect x: x y: y w: w h: h.
        brush := WinApi createSolidBrush: 16rFFFFFF.
        WinApi fillRect: hdc rect: rect brush: brush.
        WinApi deleteObject: brush.
        WinApi setBkMode: hdc mode: (WinApi constant: 'TRANSPARENT').
        WinApi setTextColor: hdc color: (WinSyntax colorRefRed: 150 green: 150 blue: 150).
        old := WinApi selectObject: hdc object: self monoFont.
        WinApi drawText: hdc
            text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: self ghostText) count: 0 - 1
            rect: rect
            format: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_LEFT')
                bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'DT_VCENTER') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'DT_SINGLELINE'))).
        WinApi selectObject: hdc object: old.
        ^1
    ]


    "── When it shows ────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> workspaceIsEmpty ^ <Boolean> [
        "WM_GETTEXTLENGTH rather than reading the text: this is asked on every
         drain pass, and the answer is one number rather than a copy of the
         whole document."
        | c |
        c := self controlNamed: (self contentNameFor: #workspace).
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^(c send: (WinApi constant: 'WM_GETTEXTLENGTH') wParam: 0 lParam: 0) = 0
    ]

    WinShell class >> ghostShouldShow ^ <Boolean> [
        "Empty, and the Workspace on screen. NOT `and unfocused`, which is
         EM_SETCUEBANNER's rule and the wrong one here: the hint is a
         TEACHING affordance, and the moment it is most wanted is exactly
         when a new user has clicked into an empty pane and does not know
         what to type. It goes on the first keystroke, which is §2.1's own
         wording."
        (self activeView == #workspace) ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        ^self workspaceIsEmpty
    ]

    WinShell class >> serviceGhost ^ <Boolean> [
        "Reconciled every drain pass and IDEMPOTENT — the same shape WG4 D4's
         enablement had to be rewritten into after it got stuck permanently
         off. A pass where nothing changed costs one WM_GETTEXTLENGTH and a
         Boolean compare, and touches no window at all.

         Answers whether it changed anything, so a gate can tell `it decided
         not to` from `it never ran`."
        | want |
        want := self ghostShouldShow.
        want = GhostVisible ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        GhostVisible := want.
        (self controlNamed: self ghostName) ifNotNil: [ :c | c show: want ].
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> ghostIsVisible ^ <Boolean> [
        ^GhostVisible == true ]

    WinShell class >> onGhostClicked [
        "Forward the click to the pane the hint is sitting on, so the hint is
         not a dead zone. Without this, clicking the one thing on screen that
         invites you to type does nothing at all."
        (self controlNamed: (self contentNameFor: #workspace)) ifNotNil: [ :c |
            WinApi setFocus: c handle ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── The old implementation, retired ──────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> installGhost [
        "WG5a D4's EM_SETCUEBANNER, replaced. Kept as a method because 93
         calls it when the Workspace is built, and turned into the thing that
         actually works: build the overlay.

         The cue banner is NOT sent any more. It returned 0 on this control —
         the refusal was always there to be read, and nobody read it."
        self buildGhost.
        ^self
    ]
]
